  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Three.js Scene**\n\nI\'ve determined that `Car3DCanvas.tsx` is, in fact, a Three.js scene. Currently, its primary function seems to be limited to modifying the `selectedPaint` property. I am focused on the user\'s desire: a customizable 3D model of the GK hypercar. The goal is to allow the user to interact with this model by altering aspects like color and tires.\n\n\n**Expanding Customization Options**\n\nNow, I\'m envisioning this Three.js scene evolving into a full car customizer. I\'m focusing on user options: body paint colors with various finishes, and rim styles with live 3D mesh swapping or updates. My target features are body colors and finish types: metallic, satin, or pearlescent. I am considering wheel styles like forged 5-spoke, aero turbine disc, and cyber mesh.\n\n\n**Defining Component Options**\n\nI am now delving into the specifics for the car components and their customization options. I\'m looking at wheels, tires, brake caliper colors, and spoilers. For example, I\'m thinking of rim styles like \'Concave Star\' with a few color options and tire styles, such as \"Sport Grooves\", or \"Cyber Glow Neon Ring\". I am also considering caliper colors and spoiler types.\n\n\n**Adding Interactive Features**\n\nMy focus has shifted to user interactions with the 3D model. I\'m prioritizing features such as underglow lighting and engine revving sounds. I\'m considering options for underglow colors like Neon Cyan, Laser Magenta, Solar Amber, and Toxic Green. Additionally, I\'m working on animating wheel spin and adding a synthesized engine rumble, all controllable via interactive buttons. I will also be working on headlights and other options.\n\n\n"
