    thinking: "**Refining Dynamic Components**\n\nI\'m making good headway building out dynamic Three.js sub-assemblies. The goal is to cleanly implement user customization. I have a clear structure now for a new wheel component in the `Car3DCanvas.tsx` structure. I am rebuilding it to allow for: body color and paint finish customization via swatches.\n\n\n**Defining Customization Options**\n\nNow, I\'m defining specific customization choices. I\'ve got a color palette mapped out, including Apex Cobalt, Track Crimson, and others, along with finishes like metallic, matte, and pearl. Next, I\'m tackling the wheel rims, focusing on designs such as forged-5 and cyber-mesh. Lastly, tires, starting with slicks and grooved options, have also been put into the design plans.\n\n\n**Expanding Feature Definitions**\n\nI\'m now detailing some new features. I\'ve designed various tire styles, like the \'neonglow\' and \'allweather\' options. Brake caliper colors, and wing options such as \'gt-wing\' and \'ducktail\', are next on the list. I\'m also including neon underglow lighting and custom headlight options. I\'m aiming for interactive play and features such as wheel spinning and sound effects.\n\n\n**Adding Interactive Controls**\n\nI\'m now integrating crucial interactive controls. I\'ve enabled a real-time wireframe toggle, a \"Randomize Custom Build\" feature for instant combinations, and a \"Reset to Factory Spec\" option. Camera presets have been designed for varied viewing angles. I\'m also planning a tabbed Customizer Control Dock below the 3D canvas, neatly organizing controls into Body & Paint, Rims & Calipers, Tires & Grip, Aero & Neon, and Play & Engage sections. I\'m now testing how wheels rotate independently.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 45
      nanos: 131581555
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
