  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Locating Draco Decoders**\n\nI\'ve successfully placed the Draco decoder files within the `public/draco/` directory. Now, in my client-side JavaScript using Vite, I\'m setting up a `DRACOLoader` and configuring it to use the \'/draco/\' decoder path. I\'m also preparing to integrate this with a `GLTFLoader` instance to load and decode GLTF files.\n\n\n**Testing Model Properties**\n\nI\'m now diving into the sports car GLTF model. I\'m exploring its materials, meshes, and scale within the Three.js environment. I\'ve determined that the car\'s bounding box has a length of ~4.5, width ~2.0, and height ~1.2 units. I\'m also working on centering the model at the origin and ensuring it sits correctly on the floor. After that, I will start by customizing the \"Body Paint\".\n\n\n**Defining Customizations**\n\nI\'m now identifying and categorizing different customizable components of the car model. I\'ve pinpointed specific parts like rims, brakes/calipers, tires, glass, carbon trim, and aero wings, each linked to their own update parameters. I\'m also planning to incorporate dynamic neon underglow and interactive LiDAR hotspots for enhanced visualization of the model.\n\n\n**Adding Camera Angles**\n\nI\'m now integrating several live camera angles to enhance the user\'s view, including a cinematic front 3/4, a side profile, a rear aerodynamic view, an interior cockpit perspective, and a top-down aerial shot. I\'m focusing on dynamic perspectives to give the most complete model view.\n\nI\'m also working to implement the colour scheme based on dark and light modes. I\'m carefully inspecting the `src/index.css` file to ensure the correct colors are applied to the primary and secondary elements, both text and background. The color assignments look good, but I\'ll have to verify correct usage.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 754010357
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
